\section{Group-wise assignment}{

\code{by} and \code{bysort} evaluate the mutation separately within groups, as
Stata's \verb{by varlist:} prefix does. Groups are formed first. Then, for
each group, \code{where} is evaluated on that group's rows and \code{values}
against that group's columns, with \code{.N} the group's row count and \code{.n}
the within-group row number. Per group, \code{values} must have size one,
the group's selected-row count, or \code{.N}; anything else is an error
naming the group's key values. The per-group results are gathered into
one full-length assignment and written through the same path as an
ungrouped call, so storage validation, compact patching, transactions,
and data.table handling are unchanged. Rows a group's \code{where} does not
select are left alone by \code{replace_values()} and hold missing after
\code{gen()}, which still appends the new column once.

This is Stata's order of operations, not data.table's. data.table's
\code{dt[i, j, by]} applies \code{i} first and groups only the surviving rows,
so under a non-empty \code{i} its \code{.N} counts selected rows and its groups
omit any group \code{i} empties. Here \code{.N} counts the group's rows whatever
\code{where} selects, and \code{where = .n == .N} marks each group's last row.
\code{.SD}, \code{.GRP}, and \code{.BY} are not provided; summaries stay with dplyr.

\code{by} groups the dataset in its current row order and never sorts.
\code{bysort} first sorts the dataset by reference on every listed column,
in Stata's total order for \verb{dta_*()} columns (finite values, then \code{.},
then \code{.a} through \code{.z}), and then groups by those same columns, so the
rows within each group are the sorted rows and \code{.n} follows the sort.
Stata's parenthesized sort-only keys are not supported: \verb{bysort id (date):} is an \code{arrange()} or \code{reorder_dta_rows()} line followed by
\code{by = id}. Group identity uses Stata value identity for \verb{dta_*()}
columns and ordinary identity otherwise, so missing values form their
own group, as in Stata, and each extended missing code its own.

A grouped tibble supplies its dplyr groups. Giving \code{by} or \code{bysort} to
one is an error rather than a precedence rule; ungroup it first.
Supplying both \code{by} and \code{bysort} is also an error. Column names follow
the package's usual rules: \code{by = g}, \code{by = c(g1, g2)}, \code{by = c("g1", "g2")}, \code{by = !!name}, and \code{by = .(name)}, where \code{.()} evaluates its
argument to a string as it does everywhere else in dtatools, not
data.table's \code{list()}.

\code{gen()} appends one variable and does not implement Stata's \code{before()} or
\code{after()} placement. The new column takes the storage in
\link{dta-storage-defaults}: a declared \verb{dta_*()} result keeps its storage,
bare integer results are \code{long}, bare double results take Stata's
\code{generate} default of \code{float}, or \code{double} under
\code{options(dtatools.generate_type = "double")}, the equivalent of Stata's
\verb{set type double}; logical results stay logical, \code{Date} and \code{POSIXct}
results keep their class with a Stata date or datetime declaration, and
character results
keep a valid declared \code{stata.string.storage} or take the smallest
\code{str1} through \code{str2045} width that fits, or \code{strL} above 2,045 UTF-8
bytes. Standard \code{haven_labelled} results preserve their label metadata.
Other classed numeric results, including \code{difftime} and
\code{bit64::integer64}, are rejected because their physical representation
does not have Stata numeric semantics; convert them first. Numeric rows
excluded by \code{where} contain system missing. Excluded string rows contain
\code{""}, Stata's string missing, and excluded logical rows \code{NA}. Wrap the
value expression in a Stata constructor to request other storage.
\code{gen()} never changes what kind of table it was handed: a tibble stays
a tibble and a data frame a data frame, with their existing columns
untouched. Only the column \code{gen()} writes is typed. Call
\code{\link[=as_dibble]{as_dibble()}} for a Stata dataset, where every column is typed.
Stata \verb{[in]} and \verb{:lblname} authoring are not supported; the
\verb{by varlist:} prefix is \code{by}/\code{bysort}.
\code{replace_values()} and \code{repl()} preserve the input R numeric value by
default. A target whose declared storage cannot hold it exactly widens
to the narrowest eligible storage, accounting for range, integrality,
and precision without reducing the column's integer capacity. See
\link{dta-storage-defaults} for the ladder. Promotion is reported as an R
message, such as \code{variable `x` was byte now int}; suppress it with
\code{suppressMessages()}. An assignment selecting no rows promotes nothing.

This precision policy intentionally differs from Stata. A \code{float}
receiving 16777217 becomes \code{double} here, preserving 16777217; Stata 18
keeps \code{float} and rounds to 16777216. A \code{byte} receiving 0.1 becomes
\code{double} here rather than Stata's rounded \code{float}. "Exact" means the
input binary64 R value, not exact decimal arithmetic: R's 0.1 is already
a binary approximation of one tenth. Promotion cannot recover digits
lost before assignment, including an explicit \code{dta_float()} conversion.

\tabular{llll}{
Input and replacement \tab Stata 18 \tab \code{promote = TRUE} \tab \code{promote = FALSE} \cr
\code{float}, 16777217 \tab \code{float}, 16777216 \tab \code{double}, 16777217 \tab \code{float}, 16777216 \cr
\code{byte}, 0.1 \tab \code{float}, rounded to float \tab \code{double}, exact input R double \tab Error \cr
}

\code{promote = FALSE} disables widening and uses the declared storage's
conversion rules. Float targets can round without an error. Integer
targets reject fractional or out-of-range values, leaving the column
unchanged. This is neither a general Stata-compatibility mode nor a
guarantee against precision loss.

For identifiers, choose sufficient storage in Stata before assignment,
for example \verb{generate long cluster = source_cluster} within the \code{long}
range, or \verb{recast double cluster} before replacing an existing column.
Widening an already rounded identifier cannot restore lost digits.
Investigate disagreements before adding casts that reproduce rounding
in R. The
\href{https://github.com/jbearak/dta-parser/blob/main/docs/r-stata-divergences.md#numeric-replacement}{intentional differences guide}
includes the executable Stata example, migration guidance, and links to
the Stata conformance probes and R tests. See also
\href{https://github.com/jbearak/dta-parser/blob/main/docs/adr/0024-promote-in-replace-values-as-stata-does.md}{ADR 0024}.
Character \code{NA} replacement values are normalized to \code{""}, Stata's string
missing value.

The table records the deliberate first-release choices relative to Stata's
\verb{generate [type] newvar = exp [if] [in]} command.

\tabular{lll}{
Topic \tab Stata \tab dtatools \cr
Existing name \tab Error \tab Error before mutation \cr
Numeric default \tab \code{float}, or \code{double} after \verb{set type} \tab \code{float}, or \code{double} under \code{options(dtatools.generate_type = "double")}; integer results \code{long} (see \link{dta-storage-defaults}) \cr
Explicit storage \tab Type prefix \tab \verb{dta_*()} value expression \cr
Strings \tab Smallest fitting \code{str#} or \code{strL} \tab Declared width, otherwise smallest UTF-8-byte width or \code{strL} \cr
Rows outside \code{if} \tab Numeric \code{.} or string \code{""} \tab Same \cr
Expression with \code{if} \tab Evaluated only for selected observations \tab Evaluated once for all rows, then selected \cr
Placement \tab Optional placement commands \tab Append only \cr
Missing report \tab Command output \tab No printed report \cr
}

Compact \code{byte}, \code{int}, \code{long}, and \code{float} columns are patched in their
native storage after validation. A direct compact target allocates work
proportional to the selected rows and does not create a full R double copy.
Newly generated compact columns retain exclusive ownership, so their first
replacement uses the same direct path without detaching a full native copy.
The native path keeps compact rollback bytes until the write commits so an
interrupt restores the original payload and missing-value cache.
A metadata proxy first detaches by copying its compact native payload so an
independent source remains unchanged; it still avoids a full R double copy.
Materialized compact numeric columns are validated against their declared
storage and patched directly in their decoded R buffer. A \code{where} expression
that is one comparison of a Stata numeric column with a scalar or another
Stata numeric column is fused with compact-target replacement, avoiding a
logical selection vector. Other selections keep the general path. Ordinary
numeric columns and character columns are patched in their existing R
representation. Replacing a dictionary-backed string materializes that
target character column, but does not copy the data frame.
Dictionary-backed replacement values are validated through a read-only
native reader, so a successful mutation, error, or interrupt does not
populate a shared source cache. \code{copy_data()} keeps unmaterialized compact
numeric and dictionary-string columns compact, and deep-copies dataset
attributes such as names and grouped-tibble metadata. It rejects columns or
attributes containing environments, functions, bytecode, external pointers,
or weak references because those objects cannot be isolated by ordinary R
copying.
}

\examples{
data <- dibble(x = dta_float(1))
repl(data, x = 16777217)
dta_storage_type(data$x)  # "double"
as.double(data$x)        # 16777217

fixed <- dibble(x = dta_float(1))
repl(fixed, x = 16777217, promote = FALSE)
dta_storage_type(fixed$x)  # "float"
as.double(fixed$x)        # 16777216

fraction <- dibble(x = dta_byte(1))
repl(fraction, x = 0.1)
dta_storage_type(fraction$x)  # "double"
identical(as.double(fraction$x), 0.1)  # TRUE

survey <- data.frame(income = c(10, 20), eligible = c(TRUE, FALSE))
gen(survey, adjusted = income + 5)
replace_values(survey, income = income * 2, where = eligible)
# The positional, Stata-shaped spelling means the same thing
gen(survey, tripled, income * 3)
replace_values(survey, tripled, 0, eligible)
independent <- copy_data(survey)
repl(independent, income = 0)

# A name known only at run time, in each position that accepts one
target_name <- "adjusted"
source_name <- "income"
repl(survey, !!target_name := 0)
repl(survey, .(target_name) := 1)
repl(survey, !!target_name, 2)
gen(survey, doubled = .data[[source_name]] * 2)
repl(survey, doubled = 0, where = .data[[source_name]] > 15)

# Group-wise assignment in Stata's `by varlist:` order
panel <- data.frame(id = c(2, 1, 2, 1), t = c(1, 1, 2, 2), x = 1:4)
gen(panel, rows = .N, by = id)               # each group's row count
gen(panel, last = .n == .N, by = id)         # each group's last row
gen(panel, above = x - mean(x), by = id)     # centred within group
repl(panel, x = 0, where = .n == 1, bysort = c(id, t))  # sorts first
}
\references{
StataCorp, \href{https://www.stata.com/manuals/dgenerate.pdf}{generate manual}.
}
